# BLUEBOOK — the language describing the language.
#
# ============================================================
# WHY THIS CHAPTER EXISTS
# ============================================================
#
# `expression.bluebook` describes the sublanguage a PREDICATE is allowed to be.
# This chapter describes everything around it : what an aggregate is, what a
# command is, what an attribute is — written in the DSL those things define.
#
# That circularity is the point. If the bluebook language can describe itself,
# then a specializer applied to this file has the parser as its subject, and the
# projection can be compared against the parser we actually wrote. Byte-identity
# is the proof that the description is complete ; any divergence is a place the
# language cannot yet say what it does.
#
#     interpreter + this chapter   →   the parser            (1st projection)
#     specializer + interpreter    →   a bluebook compiler   (2nd projection)
#     specializer + specializer    →   a compiler generator  (3rd projection)
#
# Hecks reached for this and stopped short. Its `chapters/` tree describes the
# framework in the framework — but the descriptions are an INVENTORY, not a
# specification. Across 7,270 lines it carries `emits` 92 times and `then_set`
# zero times ; `DefineAttribute(name: String, type: String)` names the concept
# and determines nothing. And there is no `chapters/bluebook.bluebook` at all —
# every chapter describing an APPLICATION was extracted, the one chapter that
# would close the loop never was.
#
# The reason is upstream of anyone's discipline : Ruby held predicates as Procs
# and their source was unrecoverable, so a self-description COULD not carry
# behaviour. Prism removes that, which is why this file can exist here and could
# not exist there.
#
# ============================================================
# WHAT WRITING IT REVEALED
# ============================================================
#
# The reason to write the description BEFORE porting Hecks's DSL is that the
# places it cannot say what it means are exactly the keywords worth porting.
# Four surfaced, and three of them are the port list already agreed :
#
#   RELATIONSHIPS.  `Aggregate` holds value objects, commands and attributes,
#   and every one of those is modelled below as a `list_of(...)` of a flat VO
#   carrying a NAME. That is not what the parser does — it holds the built
#   thing. The language has only `reference_to`, which is a cross-root pointer ;
#   it has no `has_many` for owned members, so ownership is written as a
#   name-shaped shadow of the real structure. Every `Ref` value object below is
#   a `has_many` waiting to be spelled.
#
#   DERIVATION.  `Command#creates?` is one line — `@references.nil?`. It is a
#   function of state, so it wants `derive :creates, from: ...`. With no
#   `derive`, it is written below as a `kind` attribute that `Reference` mutates,
#   which states the CONSEQUENCE and hides the RULE. A projection reading this
#   chapter would reproduce the mutation and never learn the invariant.
#
#   READ SIDE.  `IR::Bluebook#aggregate(named)`, `#verbs`, `IR::Aggregate
#   #command(named)` are lookups over held collections — queries, every one.
#   There is no `query` keyword, so the entire read half of the IR is invisible
#   to this description. What is invisible cannot be projected.
#
#   SET MEMBERSHIP.  `AggregateBuilder::RESERVED` is a constant list and the
#   shadowing rule is a membership test against it. The sublanguage admits
#   `.include?` but has no list LITERAL, so the rule cannot be stated over a
#   fixed set. The invariant below is deliberately narrower than the code, and
#   is marked where it is weaker. This one is a gap in `expression.bluebook`,
#   not in the DSL — the first found by describing rather than by using.
#
# Nothing below is stubbed to make the file look finished. Where the language
# falls short the comment says so, because the shortfall is the deliverable.
#
# ============================================================
# HOW TO READ IT
# ============================================================
#
# Each aggregate is one builder. Each command is one DSL keyword, and its
# `given`s are the rules that builder enforces while reading source. The
# lifecycle attributes (`kind`, `installed`) are not in the builders — a builder
# enforces ordering by which method has run. Written declaratively the ordering
# has to become state, and that is a faithful translation, not an embellishment.

Hecks.bluebook "Bluebook" do
  vision "The bluebook language described in itself — one aggregate per builder, one command per keyword, so a specializer can take the parser as its subject."
  core

  # ============================================================
  # BLUEBOOK — the domain declaration
  # ============================================================
  #
  # `Hecks.bluebook "Pizzas" do ... end`. Mirrors BluebookBuilder.
  #
  # This aggregate shares its domain's name, which is not an accident — it
  # exercises the one path BluebookBuilder guards explicitly : an aggregate
  # named for its domain must NOT claim the domain constant, or the module
  # becomes unreachable and takes its siblings with it. The chapter that
  # describes that rule is the chapter that triggers it.
  aggregate "Bluebook" do
    description "One domain declaration — a name, a vision, a strategic classification, and the aggregates it installs as a namespace."

    identified_by :name

    attribute :name,           String
    attribute :vision,         String, default: ""
    attribute :classification, String, default: "unclassified"
    attribute :aggregates,     list_of(AggregateRef)
    attribute :installed,      String, default: "pending"

    # GAP (relationships) — a Bluebook HOLDS its aggregates ; the builder keeps
    # the built IR objects. With no `has_many` for owned members this carries
    # names only, so the description is one indirection shallower than the code.
    value_object "AggregateRef" do
      attribute :name, String

      invariant("an aggregate reference must name something") { !name.to_s.empty? }
    end

    command "Declare" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Open a domain and give it a name"

      attribute :name, String

      emits "BluebookDeclared"
    end

    command "Envision" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "State what the domain is for, in one sentence"

      reference_to Bluebook
      attribute :vision, String

      given("a vision must say something") { !vision.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :vision, to: :vision

      emits "BluebookEnvisioned"
    end

    command "Classify" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Record Evans' strategic distinction for this domain"

      # Recorded, never enforced — it tells a reader where to spend attention
      # and later tells a generator how hard to optimise.
      reference_to Bluebook
      attribute :classification, String

      given("a domain is core, supporting, or generic") do
        classification == "core" || classification == "supporting" || classification == "generic"
      end

      then_set :classification, to: :classification

      emits "BluebookClassified"
    end

    command "AddAggregate" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Read an aggregate block and keep what it built"

      # The payload is `aggregate`, not `name` — see the IDENTITY SHADOWING note
      # in the header. This aggregate is `identified_by :name`, so a command
      # attribute called `name` is read as the identity of the Bluebook being
      # acted on and never reaches the mutation.
      reference_to Bluebook
      attribute :aggregate, String

      given("aggregates are added before the namespace is installed") { installed == "pending" }

      then_set :aggregates, append: { name: :aggregate }

      emits "AggregateAdded"
    end

    command "Install" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Name the module and lift its aggregates to top level"

      # Naming the module names every class inside it (Pizzas::Pizza) ; lifting
      # the aggregates is what lets a domain be written as plain Ruby —
      # `Pizza.create_pizza(...)` rather than `Pizzas::Pizza.create_pizza(...)`.
      reference_to Bluebook

      given("a domain with no aggregates installs nothing") { aggregates.size.positive? }
      given("a domain installs once")                       { installed == "pending" }

      then_set :installed, to: "installed"

      emits "NamespaceInstalled"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # AGGREGATE — the class-building builder
  # ============================================================
  #
  # `aggregate "Pizza" do ... end`. Mirrors AggregateBuilder, the one builder
  # that makes TWO products in a single pass : the IR, and a real Ruby class.
  # The IR is never read back to make the class — that would make Ruby a
  # projection of itself, which the arrow forbids.
  aggregate "Aggregate" do
    description "One aggregate root — its attributes, value objects and commands, plus the Ruby class constructed while the block is read."

    identified_by :name

    attribute :name,          String
    attribute :description,   String, default: ""
    attribute :identified_by, String, default: "id"
    attribute :attributes,    list_of(FieldRef)
    attribute :value_objects, list_of(TypeRef)
    attribute :commands,      list_of(TypeRef)

    # GAP (relationships) — three `has_many` collections, all flattened to names.
    value_object "FieldRef" do
      attribute :name, String

      # WEAKER THAN THE CODE. AggregateBuilder::RESERVED is
      # %i[id state events reload inspect to_h hash class] and the real rule is
      # membership in that set. The sublanguage has `.include?` but no list
      # literal, so only the load-bearing member can be stated : `state` is what
      # actually stores the attributes, so shadowing it breaks the reader it
      # would define. See the SET MEMBERSHIP gap in the header.
      invariant("an attribute may not shadow the state it is stored in") { name.to_s != "state" }
    end

    value_object "TypeRef" do
      attribute :name, String

      invariant("a type reference must name something") { !name.to_s.empty? }
    end

    command "Declare" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Open an aggregate and construct its Ruby class"

      attribute :name, String

      emits "AggregateDeclared"
    end

    command "Describe" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Say in one sentence what this aggregate is"

      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :description, String

      given("a description must say something") { !description.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :description, to: :description

      emits "AggregateDescribed"
    end

    command "IdentifyBy" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Choose the field that carries identity"

      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :field, String

      given("identity must name a field") { !field.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :identified_by, to: :field

      emits "IdentityChosen"
    end

    command "AddAttribute" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Give the aggregate a field, and the class a reader for it"

      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :field, String

      then_set :attributes, append: { name: :field }

      emits "AttributeAdded"
    end

    command "Reference" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Point at another root by its global identity"

      # `reference_to Pizza` becomes a plain `pizza_id` String — identity, never
      # an embedded object. The BARE name is used deliberately : the const shim
      # fires only for constants that do not yet exist, so once any domain has
      # installed a real `Pizza`, a later `reference_to Pizza` would receive that
      # CLASS and stringify to `OtherDomain::Pizza` — silently referring across
      # domains. A reference names a TYPE, not whichever class holds the constant.
      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :type, String

      given("a reference must name a type") { !type.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :attributes, append: { name: :type }

      emits "ReferenceAdded"
    end

    command "AddValueObject" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Read a value object block and keep what it built"

      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :type, String

      then_set :value_objects, append: { name: :type }

      emits "ValueObjectAdded"
    end

    command "AddCommand" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Read a command block, keep its IR, and define the method it describes"

      reference_to Aggregate
      attribute :verb, String

      then_set :commands, append: { name: :verb }

      emits "CommandAdded"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # COMMAND — the declaration with no body
  # ============================================================
  #
  # `command "AddTopping" do ... end`. Mirrors CommandBuilder.
  #
  # Everything a command does is DECLARED : no handler, no `def call`. The
  # runtime reads givens, mutations and emissions straight off the IR, and that
  # is precisely what makes the same declaration projectable to a language with
  # no Ruby in it.
  aggregate "Command" do
    description "One command — who issues it, what it needs, what must hold, what it changes, and what it announces. No body."

    identified_by :name

    attribute :name,       String
    attribute :role,       String, default: ""
    attribute :goal,       String, default: ""
    attribute :kind,       String, default: "creating"
    attribute :attributes, list_of(FieldRef)
    attribute :givens,     list_of(Given)
    attribute :mutations,  list_of(Mutation)
    attribute :emits,      list_of(EventRef)

    value_object "FieldRef" do
      attribute :name, String
    end

    # A rule, plus the canonical TEXT Prism recovered from its block. The text
    # is the portable form : `expression.bluebook` fixes what it may contain,
    # and both runtimes split it at evaluation time, lowest precedence first.
    value_object "Given" do
      attribute :description, String
      attribute :canonical,   String

      invariant("a rule must say what it means")   { !description.to_s.empty? }
      invariant("a rule must survive extraction")  { !canonical.to_s.empty? }
    end

    # `then_set :status, to: :new_status` replaces ; `then_set :toppings,
    # append: {...}` pushes onto a list. Two operations, no others.
    value_object "Mutation" do
      attribute :target, String
      attribute :op,     String

      invariant("a mutation must name its target")     { !target.to_s.empty? }
      invariant("a mutation either sets or appends")   { op.to_s == "set" || op.to_s == "append" }
    end

    value_object "EventRef" do
      attribute :name, String

      invariant("an event must be named") { !name.to_s.empty? }
    end

    command "Declare" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Open a command block"

      attribute :name, String

      emits "CommandDeclared"
    end

    command "Attribute" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "State a value the command needs from its issuer"

      reference_to Command
      attribute :field, String

      then_set :attributes, append: { name: :field }

      emits "CommandAttributeAdded"
    end

    command "Reference" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Mark the command as acting on an instance that already exists"

      # GAP (derivation). In the parser this is not a mutation at all —
      # `creates?` is `@references.nil?`, read fresh whenever asked. Written as
      # a `then_set` it records the CONSEQUENCE (kind becomes "acting") and
      # loses the RULE (kind IS the absence of a reference). A projection built
      # from this chapter would copy the assignment and never learn the
      # equivalence. It wants `derive :kind, from: :references`.
      #
      # The distinction is load-bearing : a creating command mints identity so
      # it becomes a CLASS method, and any other command already knows its
      # instance so it becomes an INSTANCE method and never asks for an id.
      reference_to Command
      attribute :type, String

      given("a command references one root, once") { kind == "creating" }

      then_set :kind, to: "acting"

      emits "CommandReferenced"
    end

    command "Constrain" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "State a rule that must hold before the command runs"

      # The block stays real Ruby and is never re-spelled. Prism reads its
      # source, so `toppings.size < 10` is a genuine method call at runtime AND
      # a comparison node at extraction time — both readings of one text.
      reference_to Command
      attribute :description, String
      attribute :canonical,   String

      given("a rule that did not survive extraction is not a rule") { !canonical.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :givens, append: { description: :description, canonical: :canonical }

      emits "CommandConstrained"
    end

    command "Mutate" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Declare a change the command makes to the aggregate's state"

      reference_to Command
      attribute :target, String
      attribute :op,     String

      given("a mutation either sets or appends") { op == "set" || op == "append" }

      then_set :mutations, append: { target: :target, op: :op }

      emits "CommandMutationAdded"
    end

    command "Emit" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Announce that something happened, in the past tense"

      reference_to Command
      attribute :event, String

      given("an event must be named") { !event.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :emits, append: { name: :event }

      emits "CommandEmissionAdded"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # ATTRIBUTE — the field, and the explicitness of its shape
  # ============================================================
  #
  # `attribute :toppings, list_of(Topping)`. Mirrors AttributeCollector.
  #
  # List shape is EXPLICIT and nothing is inferred from the name. `list_of(X)`
  # returns a marker the collector unwraps ; a bare type is always scalar. The
  # auto-list heuristic — plural name implies collection — was retired because a
  # domain called `address` holding several is a real thing, and a language that
  # guesses is a language that is sometimes wrong silently.
  aggregate "Attribute" do
    description "One typed field — its name, its type, whether it is a list, and its default. Shape is declared, never inferred."

    identified_by :name

    attribute :name,  String
    attribute :type,  String, default: "String"
    attribute :shape, String, default: "scalar"

    command "Declare" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Give a holder a typed field"

      attribute :name, String
      attribute :type, String

      given("an attribute must be named") { !name.to_s.empty? }

      emits "AttributeDeclared"
    end

    command "MakeList" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Declare that this field holds many of its type"

      reference_to Attribute

      given("a field becomes a list once") { shape == "scalar" }

      then_set :shape, to: "list"

      emits "AttributeMadeList"
    end
  end

  # ============================================================
  # VALUE OBJECT — the type that is its rules
  # ============================================================
  #
  # `value_object "Topping" do ... end`. Mirrors ValueObjectBuilder.
  #
  # Invariants are captured as blocks and evaluated against the CONSTRUCTED
  # value, so the bluebook reads as a rule rather than as a validation call.
  # Value objects live INSIDE the aggregate that uses them ; duplication across
  # aggregates is fine, and a file-top-level value object is forbidden.
  aggregate "ValueObject" do
    description "One value type — fields plus the rules that must hold of any value of it. Identity is the values themselves."

    identified_by :name

    attribute :name,       String
    attribute :attributes, list_of(FieldRef)
    attribute :invariants, list_of(Invariant)

    value_object "FieldRef" do
      attribute :name, String
    end

    value_object "Invariant" do
      attribute :description, String
      attribute :canonical,   String

      # The hole in Hecks's parity contract was exactly here : `dump.rs`
      # excluded invariant expressions because the predicate was a Proc and its
      # source was "unrecoverable". Invert an invariant's meaning while keeping
      # its name and Hecks's contract sees no change. Carrying `canonical` is
      # what closes it — so this rule, on this value object, is the one that
      # states why the extension exists.
      invariant("a rule must say what it means")  { !description.to_s.empty? }
      invariant("a rule must survive extraction") { !canonical.to_s.empty? }
    end

    command "Declare" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Open a value object block"

      attribute :name, String

      emits "ValueObjectDeclared"
    end

    command "AddAttribute" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "Give the value type a field"

      reference_to ValueObject
      attribute :field, String

      then_set :attributes, append: { name: :field }

      emits "ValueObjectAttributeAdded"
    end

    command "Constrain" do
      role "Domain author"
      goal "State a rule that must hold of every value of this type"

      reference_to ValueObject
      attribute :description, String
      attribute :canonical,   String

      given("a rule that did not survive extraction is not a rule") { !canonical.to_s.empty? }

      then_set :invariants, append: { description: :description, canonical: :canonical }

      emits "ValueObjectConstrained"
    end
  end
end
